[{:db/id 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" "question-a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119659 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/38/38e7866823d1b0a029cfc1d390bdcded9bbd8a79635ad80146efd600f19545dc.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1e/1ebbf14d4f46b9fa1f603e0ac116be9ba529afde630209db5fb5b982870dadec.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" "question-f5153a05-4fb4-55bc-add5-e73b5a837403"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119660 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/41/41ea03b500685eb7ff881bb67aaa2fe3f612f5aad6d19d2a3d9c0365ba5278e2.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/10/104f03abf423695444fe4fdbdc9043aed6dbbfd7dc3388115a19a4d679f1ba74.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" :question/id #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159782" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc8cd6e0-fec4-59f6-bf63-f95c9e73406a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc8cd6e0-fec4-59f6-bf63-f95c9e73406a" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc8cd6e0-fec4-59f6-bf63-f95c9e73406a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e419addd-03bc-5503-b8f2-4323b5a419e1" "answer-e773efeb-2aed-5d30-a63f-618047d3e38e" "answer-ae69705d-4393-52b2-abc9-696b673d6331" "answer-8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" "answer-e6ceea94-487e-5a46-b3ea-38f442055374"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e419addd-03bc-5503-b8f2-4323b5a419e1" :answer/id #uuid "e419addd-03bc-5503-b8f2-4323b5a419e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e773efeb-2aed-5d30-a63f-618047d3e38e" :answer/id #uuid "e773efeb-2aed-5d30-a63f-618047d3e38e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae69705d-4393-52b2-abc9-696b673d6331" :answer/id #uuid "ae69705d-4393-52b2-abc9-696b673d6331" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" :answer/id #uuid "8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6ceea94-487e-5a46-b3ea-38f442055374" :answer/id #uuid "e6ceea94-487e-5a46-b3ea-38f442055374" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1" :question/id #uuid "a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79311" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-143b59b0-133f-55a9-aba4-577093e492df" "answer-76ef7f6d-ef95-5749-93fb-20eeddd0b606" "answer-47588aca-363b-5cbe-b2d3-66854ec8696e" "answer-a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" "answer-4f6e1323-5cbb-5d05-81d7-ed37a2420f70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-143b59b0-133f-55a9-aba4-577093e492df" :answer/id #uuid "143b59b0-133f-55a9-aba4-577093e492df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76ef7f6d-ef95-5749-93fb-20eeddd0b606" :answer/id #uuid "76ef7f6d-ef95-5749-93fb-20eeddd0b606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47588aca-363b-5cbe-b2d3-66854ec8696e" :answer/id #uuid "47588aca-363b-5cbe-b2d3-66854ec8696e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" :answer/id #uuid "a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f6e1323-5cbb-5d05-81d7-ed37a2420f70" :answer/id #uuid "4f6e1323-5cbb-5d05-81d7-ed37a2420f70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" :question/id #uuid "a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" :question/math-academy-id "q-35272" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-620ffecc-6446-571f-9e30-35668717eb9d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-620ffecc-6446-571f-9e30-35668717eb9d" :answer-field/id #uuid "620ffecc-6446-571f-9e30-35668717eb9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b88d0552-0930-5b71-a537-c45316717b44" "answer-f02020d2-3fed-5ef7-a4e4-c92e5db8fab5" "answer-dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" "answer-95748133-fe39-5ce6-8d35-f5c78273de56" "answer-74a173e3-4894-57a1-b114-4cebc35d441c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b88d0552-0930-5b71-a537-c45316717b44" :answer/id #uuid "b88d0552-0930-5b71-a537-c45316717b44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f02020d2-3fed-5ef7-a4e4-c92e5db8fab5" :answer/id #uuid "f02020d2-3fed-5ef7-a4e4-c92e5db8fab5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" :answer/id #uuid "dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95748133-fe39-5ce6-8d35-f5c78273de56" :answer/id #uuid "95748133-fe39-5ce6-8d35-f5c78273de56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74a173e3-4894-57a1-b114-4cebc35d441c" :answer/id #uuid "74a173e3-4894-57a1-b114-4cebc35d441c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f5153a05-4fb4-55bc-add5-e73b5a837403" :question/id #uuid "f5153a05-4fb4-55bc-add5-e73b5a837403" :question/math-academy-id "q-70556" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6ce72d9-3525-5097-88ee-c623de804b20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6ce72d9-3525-5097-88ee-c623de804b20" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6ce72d9-3525-5097-88ee-c623de804b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eae0e3e4-3d5e-500f-a238-5ead6dabaad9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09726ccb-ffa4-5f18-9b63-38b552326b64" "answer-84c4b891-3a0a-57b7-8450-2deb8c9ca291" "answer-eae0e3e4-3d5e-500f-a238-5ead6dabaad9" "answer-b850f1a8-97df-56f1-9296-e435261baa73" "answer-afbe1ce8-7c2f-59bd-b55a-918cd14fecdd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09726ccb-ffa4-5f18-9b63-38b552326b64" :answer/id #uuid "09726ccb-ffa4-5f18-9b63-38b552326b64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84c4b891-3a0a-57b7-8450-2deb8c9ca291" :answer/id #uuid "84c4b891-3a0a-57b7-8450-2deb8c9ca291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eae0e3e4-3d5e-500f-a238-5ead6dabaad9" :answer/id #uuid "eae0e3e4-3d5e-500f-a238-5ead6dabaad9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b850f1a8-97df-56f1-9296-e435261baa73" :answer/id #uuid "b850f1a8-97df-56f1-9296-e435261baa73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afbe1ce8-7c2f-59bd-b55a-918cd14fecdd" :answer/id #uuid "afbe1ce8-7c2f-59bd-b55a-918cd14fecdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121056 :tutorial/content "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n![](images/3f/3f4139a1701c74e72ca55b662b1d5c06b02f1d3b6cab9a96b4667439dc196068.svg)\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}(H)=2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n![](images/44/44f00659592bc6ac2742af0105fa493efa93e58e8dd63ee9af56a2d90b0e513a.svg)\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0})=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n![](images/c8/c8a606f297f86b56224cb9f50ba68b826e614d4f075b396a4104e19bc77c46e7.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120056 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
